// The explanations of 0.26.3's allocator rules (front/check/alloc_rules.vl;
// docs/design/rust-surface-plan.md §2.0; docs/grammar.md, "Allocators"):
// allocation is a capability, visible in the signature.

use crate::tools::explain::Explain;

pub const ALLOC_COUNT: i64 = 3;

pub static ALLOC_TABLE: [Explain; 3] = [
    Explain {
        code: "alloc.default",
        severity: "error",
        means: "(0.26.3) A container of std written without its allocator type, `Vec<u8>`, \
            `Buf`, `HashMap<K, V>`: std's types have no default allocator (until 0.26.3 the \
            default was Gpa), so the allocator a value may grow from is in its type, and a \
            signature says what a function may allocate from. Also a type parameter bounded \
            by Allocator declared with a default (`A: Allocator = Gpa`).",
        fix: "Write the allocator: `Vec<u8, Gpa>` (main's, what the default meant), or the \
            allocator parameter of a generic item (`fn fill<A: Allocator>(v: &mut Vec<u8, A>)`); \
            a function that only reads takes a slice, `[u8]`. `volec fix --only alloc` writes \
            Gpa, except in an item generic over an allocator, where it is yours to choose.",
        example: "fn total(v: Vec<i64>) -> i64 { v.iter().sum() }\n\
            \n\
            main.vl:1:13: error[alloc.default] 'Vec' names no allocator: its 'A' is written (no default allocator type since 0.26.3) | help: the allocator is part of the type: `Gpa` (main's, what the default meant), or a generic `A: Allocator`",
    },
    Explain {
        code: "alloc.no_in",
        severity: "error",
        means: "(0.26.3) A constructor or function that allocated from an allocator nobody \
            passed (`Vec::new()`, `with_capacity(n)`, `collect()`, `format(x)`, `s.to_string()`): \
            std has none of them. Every allocation names its allocator, as the last argument \
            of the `_in` form (`Vec::new_in(a)`, `collect_in(a)`, `format_in(x, a)`).",
        fix: "Call the `_in` form with the allocator: a parameter (`gpa: Gpa`, from main's \
            `fn main(gpa: Gpa)`), or a container's (`v.alloc()`). `volec fix --only alloc` \
            writes it when one allocator value is in scope, and says so when none or several \
            are.",
        example: "let v: Vec<i64, Gpa> = Vec::new();\n\
            \n\
            main.vl:2:28: error[alloc.no_in] 'Vec::new' allocates from nothing: pass the allocator, last, to 'Vec::new_in' | help: this function's `gpa` is the allocator to pass",
    },
    Explain {
        code: "alloc.safe_impl",
        severity: "error",
        means: "(0.26.3) An impl of std's Allocator written `impl Allocator for T`: Allocator \
            is an `unsafe trait`, as in Rust. Containers' unsafe code relies on an \
            allocator's blocks, and a type made from nothing over malloc is a global \
            allocator, so its impl says so. (Any `unsafe trait`'s impl is `unsafe impl`; \
            `unsafe impl` of a safe trait, or of a type alone, is an error.)",
        fix: "Write `unsafe impl Allocator for T`, under `// UNSAFE:` (why it cannot be safe) and \
            `// SAFETY:` (where its memory comes from, why its blocks are sound). No tool \
            writes it: the comments are the author's.",
        example: "impl Allocator for Heap { .. }\n\
            \n\
            main.vl:1:1: error[alloc.safe_impl] the trait 'Allocator' is unsafe: its impl is `unsafe impl` | help: an allocator says where its memory comes from: `unsafe impl Allocator for T`, under `// UNSAFE:` (why it cannot be safe) and `// SAFETY:` (where its memory comes from, why it is sound)",
    },
];
